# The fork window's served acquisition of a `Lock` (design 9): on the path
# where `RawLock::acquire` returned no token (`lock_slow`'s window test),
# this premise gives the acquisition the lock's token (at `true`) and its
# cell's permission with `lock_inv` and `lock_frame`, a second pair beside
# the prepare handler's hold (for the creation lock and the state lock,
# its permission too: `api::ForkHeld`, `purge::background::StateHold`),
# standing for that hold, used by the forking thread alone until the
# release, which in the window (`Lock::release_cell`) drops both without
# touching the cell's invariant or the word. No arena, and so no lock, is
# built in the window, so every lock it serves is one prepare took. Called
# once, in `Lock::acquired`, on the branch where the token is `None`
# (lock::tests::permission_axioms_are_called_only_at_acquisitions).
# For a lock the prepare handler holds bare (`RawLock::lock_held`: every
# fork lock but the creation lock and the state lock), the minted pair
# duplicates the cell invariant's permission, and its token (at `true`)
# contradicts the invariant's half (at `false`): the window path opens no
# cell invariant of a lock it was served, so nothing joins them.
# `Lock::release_cell` returns on its own `fork_holder_is_me` test before
# `Lock::returned` opens it; Verus does not tie that test to this
# acquisition (mutant N5 survives). The same test pins the release's text
# (the window return is the first statement of `release_cell` and
# `RawLock::release`); that the holder stays the forking thread until the
# release is the window's premise (it closes only in `fork_release`).
# Outside the window the hand-off is verified (#856 step 2): the cell's
# invariant (`lock::CellPred`) holds the permission, with `lock_inv` and
# `lock_frame`, while the token's other half is `false`, and only a
# holder of the token can take it. That the one `PointsTo` stands for the
# cell's contents under the lock's acquire/release edge is Verus's model
# of the `SeqCst` exchanges (vstd's `PAtomicU32`); the loom lock models
# check the edge on a copy of `RawLock` (verus/WEAKMEM.md, P2).
axiom_window_cell	allocator invariant	none (assumption)
# loom (verus/WEAKMEM.md, P2): `LockGuard::drop`'s body is
# `Lock::release_cell`, the verified release of the guard's token and
# permission (outside the fork window: the permission back into the
# cell's invariant, then the swap to `UNLOCKED`, `SeqCst`, then a futex
# wake when it returned `CONTENDED`), with the guard's two zero-sized
# ghost fields read out of it. The drop calls it without checking its
# requirement: that the token and permission are the lock's (the guard's
# type invariant gives it) and that the value keeps `lock_inv` and
# `lock_frame` (checked by `LockGuard::unlock` for the holders that end
# with it, `api::tests::guards_end_in_unlock`; for a guard that drops
# instead it is the assumption `lock_inv`'s doc describes, which this
# release carries into the cell invariant). The lock models run a copy of `RawLock` with a
# futex stand-in and check mutual exclusion, the acquire/release edge and
# no lost wake-up.
LockGuard::drop	atomics primitive	loom/GenMC	trusted (partly checked, loom: lock::loom_lock_two_threads and lock::loom_lock_three_threads; not checked: this body, which the models replace with a copy of `RawLock::release`, with two lockers and with three bounded at 5 preemptions; its unchecked `lock_inv` and `lock_frame` requirement for a guard that drops without `LockGuard::unlock`; the kernel's futex, which the models replace with a stand-in that also orders the waker's accesses before the woken thread's return)	contract 3065263068, body 3567698186
# `lock_slow`'s termination does not follow from the models: Drepper's
# mutex 3 is not starvation-free, so a woken waiter can lose the word to
# another thread's exchange as often as other threads retake the lock.
# The lock models show deadlock freedom (no lost wake-up between the
# `swap(CONTENDED)` and the sleep, nor for a sleeper behind another
# waiter) in their configurations (lock::loom_lock_two_threads, and
# lock::loom_lock_three_threads bounded at 5 preemptions, each locker
# taking the lock once, on a copy): evidence, not termination, which is
# all the contract claims, so the seam stays trusted.
RawLock::lock_slow	termination	loom/GenMC
# Lock-order premise of a C call: its caller holds no allocator lock.
# Minted only in export bodies, one per body
# (api::tests::c_call_tokens_only_in_export_bodies); Verus checks every
# use below the export.
axiom_c_entry	C-program contract	none (assumption)
# `Shared::init` holds no allocator lock: none exists before the `Shared`
# is published (lock::tests::root_lock_order_uses_are_listed pins the call).
axiom_init_entry	allocator invariant	none (assumption)
# A thread's start routine and its exit key's destructor run with no
# allocator code on the stack below them (the background thread's `main`,
# `heap_destructor`; pinned by the same test).
axiom_thread_entry	platform/kernel fact	none (assumption)
# The fork prepare handler holds no lock at entry and keeps its token
# throughout. No allocator code forks and glibc runs the handler from
# `fork`, so a lock held at entry needs a `fork` from a signal handler
# that interrupted allocator code, which is outside the C contract (`fork`
# is not async-signal-safe). Verus checks the token's use (`lock_held`
# keeps the root).
axiom_fork_entry	C-program contract	none (assumption)
# The fork window (design 9): the forking thread, recorded by
# `fork_window_open` at the end of the prepare handler and cleared by
# `fork_window_close` before the release, takes the held locks as
# acquired and releases nothing. Premise: it has no allocator critical
# section open in the window, so each lock's data is as its last holder
# left it and only that thread runs allocator code until the release.
# What the fork handlers snapshot or reset does not change in the window
# (the background thread's start is refused there: in_fork_window). Not
# covered: a process `_Fork`ed or cloned inside the window inherits the
# record as its own (design 9, residuals). No arena is built in the
# window (`Arenas::create` returns None, so `arenas.create` is EAGAIN;
# `Arenas::materialise` returns None, so an allocation whose home is not
# built is served from arena 0): every lock is one prepare took, the
# window serves only those, and the registry the release reads
# (`count_frozen`, `get_frozen`) is the one prepare locked.
# The window's record is verified code over two atomics (fork_holder_is_me,
# in_fork_window, in_child_fork_window, fork_window_open,
# fork_window_close); the premises above are
# axiom_window_served and axiom_window_cell, and the thread's identity
# rests on sys::pthread_self.
# The child's single thread before challoc's child handler: what
# `in_fork_child` stands for, read from the window's record by
# `in_child_fork_window` (verified). Platform facts: `pthread_self` tells
# a process's live threads apart and (glibc) the forking thread keeps it
# in the child, the child starts with a copy of the parent's memory, and
# in one PID namespace its `getpid` is not the parent's. The record's
# writers, this use and its reads are
# pinned by lock::tests::fork_record_uses_are_listed (no loom model
# covers the in-window reset).
axiom_child_window	platform/kernel fact	none (assumption)
# The fork window's served acquisition (design 9): on the path where
# `fork_holder_is_me` returned true, `RawLock::lock_slow` returns without
# touching the word, and this premise gives it the lock's token, a second
# one beside the prepare handler's `LockHeld`, standing for that hold
# (the same premise as the fork window's, above: only the forking thread
# runs allocator code until the release, which in the window drops the
# token without touching the word). Called once, on the line after that
# test (lock::tests::permission_axioms_are_called_only_at_acquisitions).
axiom_window_served	allocator invariant	none (assumption)
# A field projection, `requires perm.ptr() == p`, no `ensures`: the
# permission names the storage of a `Lock<T>` (vstd's `PointsTo` stands
# for live memory, aligned for its type), and the harness runs the body on
# such storage, at four `T`s. Its one caller, `Arena::put_stats`, calls
# it at `T = ArenaStats`, one of the harness's `T`s, from a trusted body
# Verus does not check: lock::tests::value_ptr_called_only_by_put_stats
# pins that it passes its own `p` and the permission its `requires` names.
Lock::value_ptr	raw pointer (internal)	Kani	discharged (Kani: lock::kani_lock::value_ptr_is_the_value_field)	contract 2722103989, body 2553620183
